## The idea

Unweighted shortest path is **breadth-first search**. BFS reaches users in order of distance, so the first time we see a user we have found a shortest way to them. We record **who we came from** (the parent) for each user. Part 1 walks the parents back from `b`. Part 2 **reuses Part 1**: distance is the path length minus one. Part 3 counts, for every friend of a friend, **how many of the user's friends know them** — skipping the user and people who are already friends — then sorts.

## Step by step

1. Part 1: if either user is unknown, return an empty list. Put `a` in a queue and in a parent map (its parent is itself).
2. Take users off the queue in order. When we take off `b`, stop. Otherwise, for each friend not yet in the parent map, record its parent and queue it. Recording on **enqueue** means no user is queued twice.
3. If `b` never got a parent, there is no path → empty list. Otherwise walk parents from `b` back to `a`, adding each user, then reverse the list once (never insert at the front — that is O(n) per insert).
4. Part 2: call Part 1. Empty path → −1; otherwise the number of users minus one (the same user gives 0).
5. Part 3: for each friend `f` of the user, for each friend `ff` of `f`: skip the user and anyone already a friend; otherwise add 1 to `ff`'s mutual count.
6. Copy the counts into a list, sort by mutual count (most first) and then by id (smallest first), and return the first `k`.

## Worked example

**Input:** Friends: 1–2, 1–3, 2–4, 3–4, 4–5. ShortestPath(1, 5), then Distance(1, 5).

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| start | queue [1]; parent {1: 1} |
| take 1 | friends 2, 3 are new → parent {2: 1, 3: 1}; queue [2, 3] |
| take 2 | friend 4 is new → parent {4: 2}; queue [3, 4] |
| take 3 | friend 4 already has a parent → skip (this is why a user is never queued twice) |
| take 4 | friend 5 is new → parent {5: 4}; queue [5] |
| take 5 | it is b → stop |
| walk back | 5 → 4 → 2 → 1, reversed: [1, 2, 4, 5] |
| Distance | 4 users on the path → 3 friendships |

**Result:** [1, 2, 4, 5] and distance 3 ([1, 3, 4, 5] would be equally correct — any shortest path is fine)

## Cost

Parts 1 and 2: O(V + E) time and O(V) memory — each user is queued at most once and each friendship looked at at most twice. Part 3: O(sum of the friends' friend counts) to count, plus O(c log c) to sort the c candidates.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// Friendships are mutual. All three parts are built on one breadth-first search.
public sealed class FriendGraph {
    private readonly Dictionary<int, HashSet<int>> _adj = new Dictionary<int, HashSet<int>>();

    // Provided boilerplate: builds a symmetric adjacency from the input.
    public FriendGraph(IReadOnlyDictionary<int, List<int>> adjacency) {
        foreach (var (u, friends) in adjacency) {
            if (!_adj.ContainsKey(u)) _adj[u] = new HashSet<int>();
            foreach (var v in friends) {
                if (v == u) continue;
                _adj[u].Add(v);
                if (!_adj.ContainsKey(v)) _adj[v] = new HashSet<int>();
                _adj[v].Add(u);
            }
        }
    }

    // The shared core: BFS from `from`, remembering each user's parent, stopping once `to` is reached.
    // Returns null if `to` cannot be reached.
    private Dictionary<int, int> ParentsUntil(int from, int to) {
        var parent = new Dictionary<int, int>();
        parent[from] = from;                          // the start is its own parent
        var queue = new Queue<int>();
        queue.Enqueue(from);

        while (queue.Count > 0) {
            int u = queue.Dequeue();
            if (u == to) return parent;               // BFS order: the first time we reach `to` is shortest
            foreach (int v in _adj[u]) {
                if (parent.ContainsKey(v)) continue;  // already seen (marked when queued, not when taken)
                parent[v] = u;
                queue.Enqueue(v);
            }
        }
        return null;                                  // `to` is in a different group of users
    }

    // Part 1: the users from a to b, inclusive. Empty if there is no path or a user is unknown.
    public IReadOnlyList<int> ShortestPath(int a, int b) {
        var path = new List<int>();
        if (!_adj.ContainsKey(a) || !_adj.ContainsKey(b)) return path;

        Dictionary<int, int> parent = ParentsUntil(a, b);
        if (parent == null) return path;

        int at = b;
        while (true) {                                // walk back from b to a
            path.Add(at);
            if (at == a) break;
            at = parent[at];
        }
        path.Reverse();                               // one reverse — never Insert(0, ...) in a loop
        return path;
    }

    // Part 2: reuses Part 1. Hops = users on the path minus one; -1 if there is no path.
    public int Distance(int a, int b) {
        IReadOnlyList<int> path = ShortestPath(a, b);
        if (path.Count == 0) return -1;
        return path.Count - 1;
    }

    // Part 3: friends of friends who are not already friends, ranked by mutual friends, then by id.
    public IReadOnlyList<(int User, int Mutual)> SuggestFriends(int user, int k) {
        var result = new List<(int User, int Mutual)>();
        HashSet<int> mine;
        if (!_adj.TryGetValue(user, out mine)) return result;    // unknown user: nothing to suggest

        var mutual = new Dictionary<int, int>();
        foreach (int f in mine) {
            foreach (int ff in _adj[f]) {
                if (ff == user) continue;                         // not yourself
                if (mine.Contains(ff)) continue;                  // not someone you already know
                int seen;
                mutual.TryGetValue(ff, out seen);                 // 0 if new
                mutual[ff] = seen + 1;
            }
        }

        foreach (var pair in mutual) result.Add((pair.Key, pair.Value));
        result.Sort((x, y) => {
            if (x.Mutual != y.Mutual) return y.Mutual.CompareTo(x.Mutual);   // more mutual friends first
            return x.User.CompareTo(y.User);                                 // ties: smaller id first
        });

        if (result.Count > k) result.RemoveRange(k, result.Count - k);       // keep the top k
        return result;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: What if there are several shortest paths?**

Any one is correct; say so, and test the length and that each step is a friendship — not one exact path

**Q: Unknown user, or the same user twice?**

Unknown → empty / −1; same user → [a] / 0. Decide before prompting, and test both

**Q: Complexity of each part?**

Path and distance O(V + E); suggestions O(sum of the friends' degrees) + sorting the candidates

**Q: Why reuse Part 1 for the distance instead of a new BFS?**

One BFS to maintain. If the path allocation ever mattered, a level-counting BFS is a small, local change

**Q: Many threads query the graph at once.**

Read-only after construction → no locks; if friendships change, swap in a new immutable graph rather than locking reads

**Q: A billion users — the graph does not fit on one machine.**

Bidirectional BFS to cut the frontier; shard by user id; for suggestions, precompute offline (a friends-of-friends job) and serve from a cache

**Q: Suggestions are slow for celebrities with millions of friends.**

Cap the fan-out (sample friends), precompute for heavy users, and rank with more signals than mutual count

